<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rama II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Buddha Loetla Nabhalai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> II, Phra Phutthaloetla Naphalai, was the second king of the Chakri dynasty of Siam, reigning from 1809 to 1824. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>, he presided over a period of peace and revived trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, while the court became a centre of the arts. He was himself a leading poet, the author of a version of the Inao dance-drama, and patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu">Sunthorn Phu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rama-ii.jpg" alt="Rama II" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Rama_II_of_Siam_(Naphalai)_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rama II was born Chim in Amphawa District, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a>, a son of Luang Yokkrabat of Ratchaburi, who later became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>, and of Nak, later Queen Amarindra. When Ayutthaya fell to Burmese invaders in 1767, his father joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>'s forces to recapture it and rose to high military rank under Taksin. In 1782 his father crowned himself King of Siam, and Chim became Prince Itsarasunthon. In 1807 he was appointed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uparaja">Uparaja</a>, succeeding his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-sura-singhanat">Maha Sura Singhanat</a>, who had died in 1803. His children included Prince Thap, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>, the future Rama IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As the eldest surviving legitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>, he succeeded to the throne in 1809. No royal naming system existed at his coronation: his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Nangklao</a> later called him Loetlasulalai, and his other son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a> changed this to Loetlanaphalai. Soon after his accession Prince Kshatranichit, a surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>, rebelled as a pretender, and Prince Thap crushed the revolt. In the same year the Konbaung king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> marched into Chumphon and razed Thalang. The Thalang campaign that recaptured it was the last Burmese invasion of Thai territory.</p><p>The reign was largely peaceful and is known as the golden age of Rattanakosin literature. Rama II was himself a noted poet and artist, and poets employed at his court included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu">Sunthorn Phu</a>, author of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-aphai-mani">Phra Aphai Mani</a>, and Narin Dhibet. In 1811 the grand royal funeral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> was held, and a cholera epidemic broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. He established an education and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in nine levels and restored the Vesak festival in 1817. The first mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaqing-emperor">Jiaqing Emperor</a>, was sent in 1810, and the Portuguese governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> sent the first formal Western mission since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1818.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rama II died very suddenly in July 1824. Contemporary accounts gave strangury as the cause, but there were persistent rumours of poison. Under the succession rule in theory in force the throne would have passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>, son of the queen, but Prince Chetsadabodin, his elder half-brother, succeeded on the same day, since his experience in putting down the revolt and in the ministry of trade counted for more. The king is believed to have had over 240 grandchildren, and he had 83 children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rama II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wet-rice farming in the Chao Phraya basin fed the population, while the court profited from royal trade monopolies and from the junk trade with China, which flourished under <b>Rama II</b> and Rama III and was largely run by Chinese merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A group of Siamese traditional musicians chose &quot;Bulan Loi Luean&quot;, a composition of King <b>Rama II</b>, and Chulalongkorn ordered Christopher Hewetson, a Dutch bandmaster in the Royal Siamese Army, to arrange it in western style for military band.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ratchaburi, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.54,99.82&amp;year=1804" aria-label="Ratchaburi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rama II"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5812 3853h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5812 3853h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.54,99.82&amp;year=1804">What was here in 1804? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
